 # Product Ontology
 
-The agreed map of what a product is made of in Garura: the kinds of things, what each holds, how they connect, and what must always be true. A person and an agent reading it use the same words for the same things. It defines thirteen kinds in two layers, with the Plan as the thread between them: the **product layer** — Business Intent, Source, Domain, Capability, Feature, Slice and ICE; the **Plan**; and the **work layer** — Epic, Story, Spike, Chore and Bug. It grows one kind at a time, through the `specify-ontology` play, and the product owner agrees each change.
+The agreed map of what a product is made of in Garura: the kinds of things, what each holds, how they connect, and what must always be true. A person and an agent reading it use the same words for the same things. It defines fourteen kinds in two layers, with the Plan as the thread between them: the **product layer** — Business Intent, Source, Domain, Capability, Feature, Slice and ICE; the **Plan**; and the **work layer** — Epic, Feature issue, Story, Spike, Chore and Bug. It grows one kind at a time, through the `specify-ontology` play, and the product owner agrees each change.
 
-How it flows: a person gives an intent → it becomes a **Business Intent** here and an issue labelled `product` in the tracker → the Business Intent links to its **Capabilities** and holds no **ICE**; each Capability has **Features**, and each Capability and each Feature has exactly one ICE, its own node → a **Slice** groups Features from any Capabilities, in order, into one vertical a person can test end to end; `/design-slice` writes the link "slice serves business intent", a thin ICE for each part the slice needs that has no ICE, and the slice's hidden file, with one entry for each Feature → the plan play makes a **Plan** right after `/design-slice`: the Plan is given the Business Intent, holds every Slice that serves it, and creates one **Epic** for each, with its Stories, and writes nothing in the evals folder → the Epic holds one **Story** for each Feature of the Slice, and keeps the map from each Feature to its Story; each Story delivers its Feature and writes its scope of work from every line of its Feature's ICE and its Capability's ICE except the "Fails if" lines → a **Spike**, **Chore** or **Bug** is a child of one Story → as work finishes, the Business Intent is updated with what was done.
+How it flows: a person gives an intent → it becomes a **Business Intent** here and an issue labelled `product` in the tracker → the Business Intent links to its **Capabilities** and holds no **ICE**; each Capability has **Features**, and each Capability and each Feature has exactly one ICE, its own node → a **Slice** groups Features from any Capabilities, in order, into one vertical a person can test end to end; `/design-slice` writes the link "slice serves business intent", a thin ICE for each part the slice needs that has no ICE, and the slice's hidden file, with one entry for each Feature → the plan play makes a **Plan** right after `/design-slice`: the Plan is given the Business Intent, holds every Slice that serves it, creates one **Epic** for each, and finds or files the **Feature issue** of each Feature of the Slice that is not yet built, and writes nothing in the evals folder → the Epic holds the Feature issues, and keeps the map from each Feature to its Feature issue; each Feature has exactly one Feature issue, whose text names it (`Represents: <feature id>`) and holds its scope of work, written from every line of its Feature's ICE and its Capability's ICE except the "Fails if" lines → a **Story** is one piece of work inside one Feature issue → a **Spike**, **Chore** or **Bug** is a child of one Story → a person closes the Feature issue, and its Feature is built → as work finishes, the Business Intent is updated with what was done.
 
 The links, in five lines:
 
-1. A Plan is made by the plan play right after `/design-slice`. It is given one Business Intent; it holds every Slice that serves that intent; it creates one Epic for each Slice, with its Stories (three actions, in this order). It writes nothing in the evals folder.
-2. An Epic holds Stories; each Story sits inside exactly one Epic.
-3. A Story delivers one Feature, and with it that Feature's part of its Capability.
-4. A Spike, Chore or Bug is a child of one Story. It reaches the Feature, the Epic and the Business Intent through that Story.
-5. Domains and Capabilities are in the product model only; no work item links to them. A Story reaches its Capability through its Feature.
+1. A Plan is made by the plan play right after `/design-slice`. It is given one Business Intent; it holds every Slice that serves that intent; it creates one Epic for each Slice, and finds or files the Feature issue of each Feature of that Slice that is not yet built (three actions, in this order). It writes nothing in the evals folder.
+2. An Epic holds Feature issues; each Feature issue sits under one Epic at a time, and each Story sits inside exactly one Feature issue.
